<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu was a French naval officer and Carmelite friar who joined de Gaulle's Free French in 1940 and rose to admiral. As High Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> from 1945 to 1947 he worked to restore French rule, proclaiming the Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina in June 1946 while talks with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> were under way. His hard line contributed to the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-thierry-dargenlieu.jpg" alt="Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Conrad Poirier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:News._Commandant_Thierry_d%27Argenlieu_BAnQ_P48S1P07042.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu was born in Brest on 7 August 1889 into a family of navy officers and entered the École navale at 17. As a midshipman on the Du Chayla he took part in the campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a> in 1912, received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour and befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-lyautey">Hubert Lyautey</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, joined the Secular Order of Discalced Carmelites while on leave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1915, was promoted lieutenant de vaisseau in 1917 and the next year distinguished himself commanding the patrol boat Tourterelle in the rescue of a troop transport.</p><p>After the war he studied theology at the Angelicum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and in 1920 entered the Carmelite novitiate at Avon, taking the name Louis of the Trinity. Ordained a priest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> in 1925, he was elected Prior Provincial of the re-established province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mobilised as a reserve officer in September 1939, he was captured defending the arsenal of Cherbourg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-france">Battle of France</a>, escaped from the prisoner train after three days and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> on 30 June 1940. Meant to serve as a chaplain, he became a fighting officer by special permission of his superiors, since the Free French Naval Forces had few officers. He was badly wounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> on 23 September 1940, fired on in an unarmed boat while trying to win over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> governor.</p><p>In November 1940 he directed operations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>, and he became chancellor of the new Ordre de la Libération. He rose to counter admiral in 1941, and on 14 June 1944 he carried de Gaulle to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> aboard the destroyer La Combattante. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> on 31 October 1945 as High Commissioner.</p><p>On 23 November 1946 he ordered the French forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiphong">Haiphong</a> to make themselves complete master of the city, which led to the Haiphong Massacre, in which about 6,000 Vietnamese civilians were killed. As his actions grew more controversial he was replaced by Émile Bollaert in March 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he served as inspector general of the naval forces before retiring to a monastery. Ill, he resigned as chancellor of the Ordre de la Libération in 1958 and returned to monastic life. He died in Brest on 7 September 1964 and was buried at Avrechy. His memoirs of the war and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> were published in 1973 and 1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As France returned to Indochina after the Second World War, High Commissioner <b>Thierry d'Argenlieu</b> proclaimed the Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina on 1 June 1946, while negotiations with Ho Chi Minh's Democratic Republic of Vietnam were taking place in France.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina">Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brest, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Brest, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.39,-4.49&amp;year=1937" aria-label="Brest on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4746 5977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4746 5977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.39,-4.49&amp;year=1937">What was here in 1937? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
